We fully encourage you to use outside sources to learn more about programming,
which could include other books, websites, AI tools, and code examples.
However, some ground rules we expect you to follow, to avoid academic dishonesty:

1. All code must be your own (i.e., no copying and pasting).

2. You must understand ALL of the code, and be able to walk someone else through the logic.

3. You must cite any sources used for inspiration, guidance, debugging, etc.

4. If you use a generative AI tool like ChatGPT, you need to show the entire exchange
via a transcript.

5. If you are unsure, ask the instructors first. You want me to help you
understand the rules BEFORE you break them.

6. Citing a source costs you nothing. Academic dishonesty could cost you
your college career. Always err on the side of caution (i.e., just cite it!).

